# EntryNav-Apps @NAME@: lets it reach the car's diagnostic gateway.
# BMW's iptables-firewall sources every /etc/firewall/*.rules file at boot, after iptables.service has loaded
# /etc/sysconfig/iptables.rules, whose OUTPUT policy is DROP. This allows only outgoing HSFZ (TCP 6801) on vbr0, the
# head unit's car-side Ethernet, where the gateway is: on the link-local network, or on a laptop's DHCP network while
# it still holds that address. Replies come back through the ESTABLISHED rule already there. Each EntryNav-Apps plugin
# has its own copy of this rule, so removing one leaves the others working; a repeated ACCEPT rule does no harm.
# The first line marks this file as the plugin's. Its setup.sh install puts it in /etc/firewall; uninstall removes it.
/usr/sbin/iptables -A OUTPUT -o vbr0 -p tcp --dport 6801 -j ACCEPT
